'''Nishina clan''' (仁科氏) is a Japanese {{ill|samurai clan|ja|武家|12=draft}}. There are multiple theories about its origins, linking it to both the {{ill|Kammu Taira|ja|桓武平氏|12=draft}} (Shinano Taira) and the {{ill|Seiwa Genji|ja|清和源氏|12=draft}} (Shinano Genji).

== Shinano Nishina clan ==
=== Taira-line Nishina ===
The origins of the clan remain uncertain, with its presence first noted in historical texts around the time of the {{ill|Genpei War|ja|治承・寿永の乱|12=draft}}.

* Historian Aiji Koyama, in his work {{ill|Shinano Shigenkō|ja|信濃史源考|12=draft}}, introduces the theory that the clan originated from a branch of the ancient {{ill|Abe clan|ja|阿倍氏|12=draft}} or {{ill|Azumi clan|ja|安曇氏|12=draft}}, settling in {{ill|Azumi District, Nagano|ja|安曇郡|12=draft}} during the {{ill|Nara period|ja|奈良時代|12=draft}}. This branch managed the imperial estate {{ill|Nishina Mikuriya|ja|仁科御厨|12=draft}}.
* The {{ill|Shinpū Tōki|ja|信府統記|12=draft}} states that the clan was established after an individual named "Gorōmaru," a descendant of {{ill|Abe no Sadato|ja|安倍貞任|12=draft}} or his brother {{ill|Abe no Norito|ja|安倍則任|12=draft}}, defeated the local steward of Nishina Castle.
* According to temple records, the founder of the Nishina clan, {{ill|Taira no Kiyochika|ja|平清長|12=draft}}, took control of Nishina District in 1052 and adopted the Nishina surname.
* The {{ill|Iwashiro-Nishina genealogy|ja|岩城仁科系図|12=draft}} links the clan to descendants of {{ill|Taira no Sadamori|ja|平貞盛|12=draft}}, particularly {{ill|Taira no Chūkata|ja|平中方|12=draft}}.

=== Historical Development ===
The Nishina clan served as stewards of the {{ill|Nishina Manor|ja|仁科荘|12=draft}} under the {{ill|Imperial Household Agency|ja|宮内庁|12=draft}}. By the late {{ill|Heian period|ja|平安時代|12=draft}}, they had established themselves as a significant power in the Shinano region, with strong ties to the {{ill|Minamoto clan|ja|源氏|12=draft}}.

During the {{ill|Kamakura period|ja|鎌倉時代|12=draft}}, members of the clan fought for both the Minamoto and Emperor {{ill|Go-Toba|ja|後鳥羽天皇|12=draft}}. The clan's most notable figure, {{ill|Nishina Moriyuki|ja|仁科盛遠|12=draft}}, served as a commander under Emperor Go-Toba but perished during the {{ill|Jōkyū War|ja|承久の乱|12=draft}}.

=== Decline ===
The Nishina were later absorbed into the {{ill|Takeda clan|ja|武田氏|12=draft}}, with {{ill|Nishina Morinobu|ja|仁科盛信|12=draft}}, the fifth son of {{ill|Takeda Shingen|ja|Takeda Shingen|12=draft}}, inheriting the Nishina name. Despite their eventual fall during the {{ill|Sengoku period|ja|戦国時代|12=draft}}, some members of the clan continued as retainers under the {{ill|Tokugawa shogunate|ja|徳川幕府|12=draft}}.

