'''Nishina clan''' (仁科氏) is a Japanese {{ill|samurai clan|ja|武家|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/samurai clan|qq=|comment=enwiki is a redirect}}. There are multiple theories about its origins, linking it to both the {{ill|Kammu Taira|ja|桓武平氏|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kammu Taira|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 平氏}} (Shinano Taira) and the [[:en:Seiwa Genji|Seiwa Genji]] (Shinano Genji).

== Shinano Nishina clan ==
=== Taira-line Nishina ===
The origins of the clan remain uncertain, with its presence first noted in historical texts around the time of the [[:en:Genpei War|Genpei War]].

* Historian Aiji Koyama, in his work {{ill|Shinano Shigenkō|ja|信濃史源考|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shinano Shigenkō|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}}, introduces the theory that the clan originated from a branch of the ancient {{ill|Abe clan|ja|阿倍氏|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Abe clan|qq=}} or {{ill|Azumi clan|ja|安曇氏|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Azumi clan|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 阿曇氏}}, settling in {{ill|Azumi District, Nagano|ja|安曇郡|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Azumi District, Nagano|qq=}} during the [[:en:Nara period|Nara period]]. This branch managed the imperial estate {{ill|Nishina Mikuriya|ja|仁科御厨|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Nishina Mikuriya|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 仁科神明宮}}.
* The [[:en:Shimpu-tōki|Shinpū Tōki]] states that the clan was established after an individual named "Gorōmaru," a descendant of [[:en:Abe no Sadato|Abe no Sadato]] or his brother {{ill|Abe no Norito|ja|安倍則任|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Abe no Norito|qq=}}, defeated the local steward of Nishina Castle.
* According to temple records, the founder of the Nishina clan, {{ill|Taira no Kiyochika|ja|平清長|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Taira no Kiyochika|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}}, took control of Nishina District in 1052 and adopted the Nishina surname.
* The {{ill|Iwashiro-Nishina genealogy|ja|岩城仁科系図|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Iwashiro-Nishina genealogy|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}} links the clan to descendants of [[:en:Taira no Sadamori|Taira no Sadamori]], particularly {{ill|Taira no Chūkata|ja|平中方|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Taira no Chūkata|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}}.

=== Historical Development ===
The Nishina clan served as stewards of the {{ill|Nishina Manor|ja|仁科荘|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Nishina Manor|qq=}} under the [[:en:Imperial Household Agency|Imperial Household Agency]]. By the late [[:en:Heian period|Heian period]], they had established themselves as a significant power in the Shinano region, with strong ties to the [[:en:Minamoto clan|Minamoto clan]].

During the [[:en:Kamakura period|Kamakura period]], members of the clan fought for both the Minamoto and Emperor [[:en:Emperor Go-Toba|Go-Toba]]. The clan's most notable figure, {{ill|Nishina Moriyuki|ja|仁科盛遠|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Nishina Moriyuki|qq=}}, served as a commander under Emperor Go-Toba but perished during the [[:en:Jōkyū War|Jōkyū War]].

=== Decline ===
The Nishina were later absorbed into the [[:en:Takeda clan|Takeda clan]], with [[:en:Nishina Morinobu|Nishina Morinobu]], the fifth son of {{ill|Takeda Shingen|ja|Takeda Shingen|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Takeda Shingen|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}}, inheriting the Nishina name. Despite their eventual fall during the {{ill|Sengoku period|ja|戦国時代|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sengoku period|qq=}}, some members of the clan continued as retainers under the {{ill|Tokugawa shogunate|ja|徳川幕府|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tokugawa shogunate|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 江戸幕府}}.

